Dc6TGljZW5zZTQ="},"allow_forking":true,"is_template":false,"web_commit_signoff_required":false,"topics":["ast","ecmascript","estree","javascript"],"visibility":"public","forks":73,"open_issues":28,"watchers":550,"default_branch":"master","permissions":{"admin":false,"maintain":false,"push":false,"triage":false,"pull":true}},"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/estools/escope/pulls/120","html_url":"https://github.com/estools/escope/pull/120","diff_url":"https://github.com/estools/escope/pull/120.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/estools/escope/pull/120.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"The first 3 commits bring this repo up-to-date:\r\n\r\n* require-dir is broken with node 8 -> update gulp-git\r\n* node LTS minimium is 4 -> adjust travis\r\n* `({a}) = b` is incorrect syntax -> `({a} = b)` instead\r\n\r\nThe last commit is the important one.\r\n\r\nBefore a ClassDeclaration creates two variables, one in the outer scope and one in the inner scope. This results in weird behavior when the class is referenced from inside the class. i. e. when renaming a variable.\r\n\r\nAfter the change only a single variable is created and all references are attached to this one.\r\n\r\nNote: that a comment on one test assumes it's the correct behavior to create to two variables. I'm not this opinion. FunctionDeclarations are similar and also create only a single variable.\r\n\r\nfixes webpack/webpack#5000","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/estools/escope/issues/120/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/estools/escope/issues/120/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null}},"event":"cross-referenced"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack/issues/comments/306269250","html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack/issues/5000#issuecomment-306269250","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack/issues/5